  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29382v1</id>
    <title>RadioDreamer: A World Model for Radio Map Forecasting and Imagination-Based Optimization</title>
    <updated>2026-10-21T08:10:49Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29382v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.29382v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Radio maps characterize the spatial distribution of wireless conditions and are fundamental to network planning and optimization. However, existing learning-based methods reconstruct isolated radio-map snapshots and do not model how network actions drive the evolution of the radio environment. In this paper, we present RadioDreamer, a two-stage world model that unifies action-conditioned radio-map forecasting and imagination-based network optimization within a latent space. To learn causal environmental transitions from sequential interactions, we introduce a structured spatio-temporal transformer backbone coupled with dynamic-region supervision, enabling accurate modeling of action-sensitive areas. Second, rather than relying on conventional physical trial-and-error, RadioDreamer establishes a general planning paradigm that generates counterfactual latent trajectories to evaluate and optimize long-horizon network parameter configurations. Experiments show that RadioDreamer improves both multi-step rollout forecasting and closed-loop network optimization. It reduces full-map forecasting error by 25.2% on average and achieves approximately 80% accuracy in predicting the evolution direction of radio signals. More importantly, for long-horizon network configuration, imagination-based planning surpasses model-free policies optimized directly on radio-map observations by 8.8% in accumulated return.</summary>
    <category term="cs.NI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-10-21T08:10:49Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>10 pages, 7 gigures, 5 tables</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.NI"/>
    <author>
      <name>Haoye Chai</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Mugen Peng</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
